tten grievances, highlighting the complexity of social interactions and economic transactions in a burgeoning urban society. The tablet was inscribed by a man named Nanni, who addressed his complaints to Ea-nāṣir, a local official, illustrating the existence of a bureaucratic structure in Babylonian society and the role of writing as a tool for communication and record-keeping.

